      "coverage": {
        "learning_objective": "Recognize that a dataset is organized as rows and columns, and identify what a row and a column each represent in a simple dataset.",
        "required_facts": [
          {
            "id": "f1",
            "statement": "A dataset is a collection of observations recorded on the same variables.",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f2",
            "statement": "Data is commonly organized as a table (data matrix) of rows and columns.",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f3",
            "statement": "In a data table (data matrix), each row represents one observation (one record).",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f4",
            "statement": "In a data table (data matrix), each column represents one variable: one attribute that is recorded for every observation.",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.1"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "misconceptions": []
      },
      "teaching_blocks": [
        {
          "id": "tb1",
          "purpose": "intro",
          "content": "Picture a week's worth of delivery orders for a small courier company: order numbers, the city each order went to, how long the delivery took, which driver handled it. Written down, all of that information becomes what analysts call a dataset -- a collection of observations that are all recorded on the same set of variables. Every delivery in the batch was recorded using the same kind of information: an order number, a city, a delivery time, a driver. That shared structure is what turns a pile of individual facts into one dataset you can work with.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb2",
          "purpose": "definition",
          "content": "That information is commonly organized as a table -- sometimes called a data matrix -- made of rows and columns. If you opened the delivery-orders file in a spreadsheet, you would see exactly this: a grid where every delivery has its own line, and every piece of information about a delivery has its own column.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb3",
          "purpose": "mechanism",
          "content": "The two directions of that grid mean different things. Each row represents one observation -- one record. In the delivery data, that means one row per delivery: order #1042 is one row, order #1043 is the next row, and so on. Each column represents one variable: one attribute that is recorded for every observation in the dataset. So the city column holds the destination city for every single delivery, and the delivery-time column holds the delivery time for every single delivery -- each column holds just one kind of information.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f3",
            "f4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb4",
          "purpose": "example",
          "content": "Reading the table this way tells you exactly how to answer a question about it. Asked 'how many deliveries went to Lagos?', you would scan down the city column and count matching rows. Asked 'what happened with order #1042?', you would look across that one row and read every column for it. The row tells you about one delivery; the column tells you about one kind of fact across all deliveries.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f3",
            "f4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb5",
          "purpose": "takeaway",
          "content": "This row/column structure is the foundation everything else in analysis builds on: before you can check, clean or make sense of delivery data, you first have to know that a row is one delivery and a column is one attribute of every delivery.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f1",
            "f2",
            "f3",
            "f4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "video_need": {
