      "coverage": {
        "learning_objective": "Given a column from a dataset, classify it as categorical or numerical.",
        "required_facts": [
          {
            "id": "f1",
            "statement": "A numerical variable takes numerical values for which it is sensible to add or subtract.",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f2",
            "statement": "A categorical variable's values are categories (called levels).",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.2"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "misconceptions": [
          {
            "id": "m1",
            "statement": "A column stored as numbers is not automatically numerical: if arithmetic on the values would be meaningless (for example, a telephone area code), the column is categorical.",
            "source_refs": [
              "openintro_statistics_textbook#1.2.2"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "teaching_blocks": [
        {
          "id": "tb1",
          "purpose": "intro",
          "content": "Back in the delivery-orders table, look at two of its columns: delivery time (say, 32 minutes) and city (say, 'Lagos'). Both are columns in the same table, but they behave completely differently, and knowing which kind of column you're looking at changes what you're allowed to do with it.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f1",
            "f2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb2",
          "purpose": "definition",
          "content": "A numerical variable takes numerical values for which it is sensible to add or subtract -- delivery time is numerical, because '32 minutes plus 41 minutes' or the difference between two delivery times both mean something real. A categorical variable's values are categories, also called levels -- city is categorical, because its values ('Lagos', 'Abuja', 'Kano') are just labels for a group; there is nothing to add or subtract between them.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f1",
            "f2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb3",
          "purpose": "misconception",
          "content": "A column stored as numbers is not automatically numerical. If arithmetic on the values would be meaningless, the column is categorical even though it looks like numbers. In the delivery data, a zone_code column might hold 101, 102, 103 -- but adding two zone codes together produces nothing useful, the same way adding two telephone area codes would. zone_code is a label, not a quantity, so it's categorical despite being written as digits.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "m1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tb4",
          "purpose": "takeaway",
          "content": "Before doing anything with a column, check which kind it is: can these values be meaningfully added or subtracted, or are they just labels for groups? Getting that distinction right up front decides whether treating that column as a quantity will make sense at all.",
          "fact_ids": [
            "f1",
            "f2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "video_need": {
